+  // Cheap "is this plausibly the same object?" gate for a BlasEntry reuse that would
+  // otherwise be justified by a material-hash match alone. Required because SR3 (Saints
+  // Row: The Third) batches world decals into a dynamic vertex-buffer ring and rebuilds an
+  // entirely unrelated batch of geometry into it every frame. Measured over two 60-frame
+  // bursts in-game: index-hash flips = 0 and geometry-descriptor flips = 0 (so
+  // rules::TopologicalHash - which buckets solely on those, not positions/texcoords - stays
+  // stable, so these unrelated batches collide in ONE DrawCallCache bucket), texcoord
+  // flips = 2,416 over 2,165 tracked draws (so rules::VertexDataHash never matches), and
+  // material/albedo flips = 20 (the material hash IS stable, because many unrelated decal
+  // batches share one atlas material). With vertexDataMatches permanently false and
+  // materialHashesMatch permanently true for these draws, the bare materialHashesMatch term
+  // in DrawCallCache::get() was enough to rebind this frame's draw to a completely
+  // unrelated BlasEntry, which drags that BlasEntry's stale previous-frame positions along
+  // via SceneManager::onSceneObjectUpdated's historyBuffer[0]/[1] swap - producing bogus
+  // motion vectors and visibly stretched decals for one frame.
+  //
+  // A material-hash-only match must therefore also pass this gate: same vertex/index
+  // counts, and a world centroid within RtxOptions::uniqueObjectDistance() of the cached
+  // entry's last known position. A genuinely animated/skinned mesh keeps its vertex/index
+  // counts and moves only a little frame to frame, so it still passes; a recycled batch
+  // slot generally has an unrelated triangle count and/or an unrelated centroid, so it is
+  // correctly rejected and gets a fresh BlasEntry instead. Do NOT fold this back into a
+  // bare materialHashesMatch/getHash()== check - that regresses to the bug above.
+  bool shapeMatches(const DrawCallState& drawCall, const DrawCallState& cachedInput) {
+    if (drawCall.getGeometryData().vertexCount != cachedInput.getGeometryData().vertexCount ||
+        drawCall.getGeometryData().indexCount != cachedInput.getGeometryData().indexCount) {
+      return false;
+    }
+
+    // THE SHARPEST OF THE THREE TESTS, and the reason the other two are not sufficient on
+    // their own: require the TEXCOORD component hash to match.
+    //
+    // The counts test above does NOT catch SR3's worst offenders. The same dynamic-ring slot
+    // is refilled with a different batch that happens to carry the SAME triangle count
+    // (measured: repeated flips at prims=184 / vertexCount=368 with an identical index hash
+    // and changed texcoords), so counts match and only the 300-unit centroid test stands -
+    // and two decal batches within one city district can easily sit closer than that.
+    //
+    // Texcoords separate the two populations cleanly, which is exactly what was measured
+    // over two 60-frame bursts:
+    //     class decal/world : texcoord flips = 2,416   (recycled batch slots)
+    //     class skinned-FF  : texcoord flips = 0       (animated meshes)
+    //     class skinned-CPU : texcoord flips = 0       (animated meshes)
+    // A skinned or animated mesh deforms its POSITIONS every frame but its UVs are static,
+    // so it still passes. A recycled batch slot holds different decals with different UVs,
+    // so it is rejected.
+    //
+    // Deliberately strict: a mesh that genuinely rewrites its texcoords per frame (UV
+    // scrolling written into the vertex buffer rather than via a texture transform) is
+    // rejected too and gets a fresh BlasEntry. That costs it a BLAS build and its motion
+    // vectors for that frame, which is the safe direction to fail - the alternative is
+    // inheriting an unrelated batch's previous-frame positions and smearing it across the
+    // screen. Spritesheet animation is unaffected; Remix drives that from the surface, not
+    // by rewriting vertex texcoords.
+    if (drawCall.getGeometryData().hashes[HashComponents::VertexTexcoord] !=
+        cachedInput.getGeometryData().hashes[HashComponents::VertexTexcoord]) {
